{
  "kata": "iterator",
  "law": "Declared-sequence traversal law: with a declared finite sequence Element, a declared traversal Step (in emission order), emission arrow yields : Step -> Element and documented inverse back : Element -> Step, the traversal is a bijection onto the declared sequence - yields total, yields injective, yields;back = id_Step, back;yields = id_Element - i.e. every declared element is emitted exactly once. The order clause succ_s;yields = yields;succ_e is separate and needs declared successor cells.",
  "comparison_rule": "An element is one declared Element row, spelled element:<row name>. The baseline identifies exactly the elements its failed pre-registered position assertions name (both the expected and the actual side). The probe maps a finding to elements: a key collision names a shared emission (yields of the witness step rows); a dangling on `back` names that Element row; a violation names the element of its equation's domain row (Step-domain rows through yields, Element-domain rows directly). unique = sorted(probe elements) - sorted(baseline-identified elements). An unmappable finding is recorded verbatim and never counted.",
  "baseline_protocol": "Scripted smoke walk over a pre-registered position sample: for each sampled index i, assert emitted[i] == declared[i] and name both sides on failure; an index past the declared sequence names the unexpected emission. No total-length, termination, or tail assertion. The baseline arm runs before the probe arm inside prototype/iterator_baseline.py.",
  "element_vocabulary": "element:<Element row name>",
  "entries": [
